.\" Manual page for detc.
.\"
.\" roff, and not markdown put through a converter, which is the whole reason it
.\" is checked in looking like this.  A converter is a build dependency that
.\" every packager then has to carry for a file that changes twice a year, and
.\" the alternative -- generating it at release time and shipping the result in
.\" the tarball -- is a generated file in the source tree.  Neither is worth it
.\" for three pages.  `man ./man/detc.8` renders this one from the repository.
.\"
.\" Two fields here go stale by hand: the date and the version in .TH.
.TH DETC 8 2026\-08\-04 "detc 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.
.SH NAME
detc \- declarative generation of configuration files in a running host
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B detc
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B detc
builds a namespace of variables out of the documents installed in the system
and the output of its probes, and uses that namespace to instantiate the
.I templates
that describe configuration files and the
.I resources
that describe state which is not a file.  The distribution ships the defaults,
the administrator adjusts them, and what a machine is handed on its first boot
contributes too.
.PP
A run is a plan and not a script.
.B detc
renders every template, asks every provider what the system currently looks
like, compares that with what the objects declare, and changes only what
differs.  A second run immediately after the first changes nothing.
.PP
Every applying run is written to a git repository under
.IR /var/lib/detc ,
in two commits \(em what was found, and what was applied \(em so what the
system was yesterday is a question with an answer.  See
.BR "detc report" .
.
.SS Objects
Five kinds of thing make up a system, and all five are discovered the same way.
.TP
.B variable
A JSON, YAML or TOML document merged into the namespace.
.TP
.B probe
An executable that writes such a document to its standard output, so that a
fact about the machine is a variable like any other.
.TP
.B template
A MiniJinja file whose rendered content is a configuration file.
.TP
.B resource
A document that declares a piece of state which is not a file \(em a package, a
unit, a user.
.TP
.B provider
An executable that implements one type of resource.
.PP
Discovery follows the UAPI Configuration File Specification: a
.IR name .d
drop\-in directory, searched in several prefixes, lowest priority first.
.PP
.EX
what is read        usr/share    run       etc
what is executed    usr/libexec  run/lib   var/lib
.EE
.PP
Priority grows to the right, so what the administrator installs always wins
over what the distribution ships and over what was injected into the machine
from outside.  Within a prefix the drop\-ins are applied in lexicographic
order, hence the
.IR 10\- / 50\- / 90\-
naming, an entry of the same name in a higher prefix overrides the one below
it, and
.B a zero byte file masks it entirely.
.PP
The middle column is where a bundle lands; see
.BR "detc bundle" .
.
.SH OPTIONS
Global, so they go before the subcommand.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-root " " \fIPATH\fR
Work on a system mounted somewhere else instead of
.IR / .
Every path above is taken under it, and nothing outside it is read or written.
Useful to prepare an image, and the only way to try something out without
touching the host.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Say what would happen instead of doing it.  It covers everything that writes:
.BR apply ,
.BR remove ,
.BR unmask ,
the
.B var
invocations that set a variable, and
.BR "bundle install" ,
.B "bundle restore"
and
.BR "bundle remove" .
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Turn logging on, and repeat for more of it: error, warn, info, debug, trace.
Nothing is reported by default.
.B DETC_LOG_LEVEL
overrides this.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help, for the command or for a subcommand named after it.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Overriding a variable for one command
.BR apply ,
.BR cat ,
.B check
and
.B var
take the same three, which put a value into the namespace for that command
alone and write nothing down.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIKEY\fR
A dotted key, such as
.IR ssh.permit_root_login .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-value " " \fIVALUE\fR
Its value, in YAML syntax, so
.I 22
is a number and
.I "'22'"
is a string.
.TP
.BI \-\-kv " KEY=VALUE"
Both at once, in YAML syntax.  Repeatable.
.
.SS detc apply [FILE]
Bring the system to the state its objects declare.  With no
.IR FILE ,
everything; with one, the configuration file or the resource of that name
alone.
.B \-t
.I TYPE
narrows it to one type, which can only be
.B template
or
.BR resource :
a probe, a provider and a variable document are what the objects that are
applied are made of, and are not applied themselves.
.PP
What is planned is only what differs, in the order the providers declare, and
each change is reported as it is made.  Objects that could not be applied are
reported and the run ends with a non\-zero status; the ones that succeeded are
still applied and still recorded.
.PP
A machine that kept a bundle and does not hold it \(em one that has rebooted and
has not converged yet \(em has it put back here, before anything is measured, so
that what the system declares is all of what it declares.  A restore that fails
fails the run.
.PP
With
.B \-\-dry\-run
nothing is put back, and what the ladder does not hold is not planned either, so
the report that follows is measured without the bundle: on a machine that is
missing everything one carries it would otherwise be an empty report, which
reads as nothing to change.  The first line says which it is.
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ detc \-\-dry\-run apply
restore  bundle fleet 3  would be put back first, so what follows is measured without it
.EE
.RE
.
.SS detc list
List the objects of the system: what they are, the name that addresses each,
and the file it was read from.
.TP
.B \-\-types
List the types instead of the objects.
.TP
.B \-\-masked
List the objects that a zero byte file takes out of the ladder instead, with
the file that does the masking in the last column rather than the file it
hides.
.IP
These are names that no other command can see: the resolver reads a zero byte
file as the object being absent, so a masked object is in no other listing,
renders nothing, and is reached only by
.BR "detc unmask" .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
List only that type.
.
.SS detc cat OBJECT
Show what
.I OBJECT
holds: the content a template would write, instantiated against the namespace
of this machine, the declaration of a resource expanded the same way, the
program that a probe or a provider is, or a variable document as it was
written.
.PP
.I OBJECT
is addressed the way
.B detc list
prints it, and either column of that line will do: the file a template
instantiates, the
.IR type / name
of a resource, the mount point a probe feeds or its path, the type a
provider implements or its path, and the
.IR group / name
of a variable document.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Show the template or the declaration as it was written, before the variables
reach it.  A probe, a provider and a variable document are never expanded, and
are always shown as they are.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
Which type
.I OBJECT
names, guessed from the name when it is left out.  A type also reads a program
that is not installed as one, which is how a probe is tried before it is
shipped.
.
.SS detc check [FILE]
Check that the objects can be instantiated: that every variable document
parses, that every probe answers with a document, that every template renders,
that every resource matches the schema of its provider, and that every provider
is there and answers.  It changes nothing and needs no privilege beyond
reading.
.PP
The status is non\-zero if anything failed, which is what makes this the
command to put in a pipeline.
.
.SS detc remove OBJECT...
Take objects away, and say what that uncovers.
.PP
Each
.I OBJECT
is addressed the way
.B detc cat
addresses it, and what is unlinked is the file that the ladder resolved \(em
never a path that was typed.  One object is one file in every type, so a removal
is always an unlink and never a document rewritten with a piece left out.
.PP
Taking a file out of a ladder is not deleting an object, it is uncovering
whatever was under it.  A copy in a lower prefix answers for the name from then
on, and is reported as
.BR remains ,
with the file it comes from.  Nothing is said when the name no longer addresses
anything, because then the object really has gone.
.PP
Three of the types leave something in the system when they go, and it is
reported as
.BR orphan :
a template leaves the configuration file it wrote, which goes on configuring the
machine with nothing left to say where it came from; a provider leaves every
resource of its type, which nothing can apply any more; and a resource leaves
whatever its provider did about it, which is a package that is installed or a
unit that is enabled, and which nothing manages now.  For a template the line
says whether the file is still
.B as detc wrote it
or has
.B changed since detc wrote it
\(em and, when the template can no longer be instantiated, that the two cannot
be told apart.  A file that was never written is not reported.
.PP
For a resource the line says only that
.BR "nothing manages what it declared any more" ,
and not what the state now is.  Finding that out means building the namespace
and inspecting, which is a run of every probe on the machine; and unmanaged is
the fact either way, whether the declaration was ever applied or not.  A probe
and a variable document leave nothing: what they contribute exists only while
they are read.
.PP
Every object is resolved and judged before any of them is touched, so a command
naming several is never half done.
.PP
The lock is taken, since a run that is halfway through instantiating an object
must not have it unlinked underneath it.  A removal is not written to the
journal: it changes what the system would do, not what it is, and the next
.B detc apply
records the difference.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
Which type the objects are, guessed from the name when it is left out.  Unlike
.BR "detc cat" ,
a type reaches only what the ladder installed: a file that is merely on the
machine is not an object to take away.
.TP
.B \-\-mask
Write the zero byte file in the administrator's prefix instead of unlinking
anything, which the resolver reads as absent.
.IP
This is the only way to be rid of what the distribution ships \(em unlinking
that is refused, because
.B detc
does not write
.I usr/share
or
.I usr/libexec
and the next upgrade would put the file back anyway.  It is also the answer for
a file that the installed bundle owns, which unlinking would only take away
until the next restore or the next boot; that too is refused, naming the bundle.
Masking something that is already in the administrator's prefix is refused as
well, there being no prefix above it to write in.
.IP
A mask is an ordinary file, and
.B detc unmask
is what takes it away again.
.B detc remove
cannot, since a masked object is no longer an object.
.TP
.B \-\-purge
Also take away the file that a template instantiated, when nothing else
instantiates it and it still holds exactly what the template wrote; the line is
then
.B purge
rather than
.BR orphan .
A file that was edited, or one whose template no longer renders and so cannot be
compared, is named and left where it is \(em deleting somebody's work on a guess
is the one mistake a removal must not make.
.IP
It is refused for every other type, and for two reasons.  A probe, a provider
and a variable document write nothing into the system, so there is nothing of
one to take away.  A resource does leave something, but absence is a state a
declaration asks for like any other, and reaching it is what
.B detc apply
does: declare the resource absent, apply that, and take the declaration away
once the system says so.  Which property spells absent belongs to the type \(em
.I installed: false
for
.BR pkg ,
.I ensure: absent
for
.B path
\(em and the schema that
.B detc doc \-\-type provider \fITYPE\fR
prints is where it says so.
.IP
A purge is the one thing a removal does that reaches the history, since it is
the one thing that changes the system rather than what the system would do.  It
is committed as a single commit rather than the pair that
.B detc apply
writes, a file that is gone having no before and after to compare, and
.B detc report
names it under the
.B remove
command.  A removal without
.B \-\-purge
records nothing, and the next
.B detc apply
records the difference.  The dump in
.I /var/lib/detc/last.yaml
is left alone either way: it is the report of the run that reconciled the
system.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Global, and named here because it can say less than usual.  What would be
unlinked or masked is named, but what that would uncover is a question about the
ladder without the file in it, and the only honest way to ask it is to take the
file out; so there is no
.B remains
line and no verdict on an orphan.  With
.B \-\-purge
the file that is at stake is still named.
.
.SS detc unmask OBJECT...
Put objects back that a zero byte file takes out of the ladder.
.PP
This is the other end of
.BR "detc remove \-\-mask" ,
and the one command that addresses an object the resolver reads as absent.
Each
.I OBJECT
is named the way
.B detc list \-\-masked
prints it \(em either column of that line will do, so the path of the zero byte
file is enough, which is what
.B detc remove \-\-mask
printed when it wrote the thing.
.PP
The
.B unmask
line names the mask that was unlinked, and one line follows saying what the
ladder answers for the name now.  That is
.B remains
with the file that was under the mask;
.B masked
when a second zero byte file was under the first \(em an installed bundle can
carry one of its own \(em and still hides what is below it; or
.B absent
when the mask covered no file at all, which is what is left when the file it was
written against went away.
.PP
A mask that the distribution ships is not
.BR detc "'s"
to unlink, and neither is one that a bundle installed.  Unlike a removal there
is no second answer to offer for either, since a mask cannot itself be masked.
.PP
Every mask is resolved and judged before any of them is touched, so a command
naming several is never half done.  The lock is taken, and an unmask is not
written to the journal, for the same reasons a removal is not.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
Which type the objects are, guessed from the name when it is left out.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Global, and named here because it can say less than usual.  The mask that would
be unlinked is named, and nothing more: what putting the object back would
uncover is a question about the ladder without the mask in it.
.
.SS detc doc OBJECT
Show what
.I OBJECT
says about itself: the block of comments at the head of its file, with the
comment sign taken off.  The block ends at the first line that is neither a
comment nor blank, and a shebang is not part of it.
.PP
The documentation lives in the object and not in a catalogue that
.B detc
keeps, so whoever changes a probe or a template changes what it says, and a
bundle brings the documentation of what it carries with it.
.PP
.I OBJECT
is addressed the way
.B detc cat
addresses it.  For a provider the schema it publishes is appended to what its
header says, under a
.B ## Schema
heading and indented as an example, the way the header sets off one of its
own \(em what a resource of that type may declare is part of describing it.
.B detc schema
is the same document unindented, for a script.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " " \fITYPE\fR
Which type
.I OBJECT
names, guessed from the name when it is left out.
.
.SS detc schema PROVIDER
Ask
.I PROVIDER
for the schema of what it accepts, and show the document as it wrote it.  It
is not written down anywhere in
.BR detc :
the provider is asked.
.PP
.I PROVIDER
is the type of resource it implements, as
.B detc list \-\-type provider
prints it, or the path of the program \(em which is how one is read before the
system has installed it.
.PP
This is the schema on its own, for a script;
.B detc doc
is the same thing under the prose the provider carries, for a person.
.
.SS detc var [FILE]
Query the namespace, or set part of it.
.PP
This is the namespace and not the documents it was built from.  A key holds one
value here, whichever document won it, and the ones that lost are not in the
answer at all.
.B "detc list \-\-type variable"
shows the documents themselves, and
.B "detc cat"
shows one of them as its author wrote it.
.PP
With
.B \-k
alone it prints the value of that key.  With
.B \-k
and
.BR \-v ,
or with
.BR \-\-kv ,
it writes the override to
.I /run/detc/variables/user.d
so that the next run reads it and the next boot takes it away.  With a
.IR FILE ,
it merges that whole document into the same place.  With none of them it
prints the namespace.
.TP
.B \-\-persist
Write what is set to
.I /etc/detc/variables/user.d
instead, where a reboot cannot reach it, and take away the runtime drop-in that
it replaces.
.TP
.B \-\-unset
Take away the drop-ins written for the keys given with
.BR \-k ,
in both directories at once, instead of setting anything.  Only the drop-ins
named after the key are reached, so a document written by hand and a document a
bundle installed are left alone; a key that no drop-in holds is not a failure.
Taking a drop-in away uncovers whatever was under it, so what still answers for
the key is reported as
.BR remains ,
with the file it comes from or
.B "a probe"
for a value that the machine reports about itself.
.TP
.B \-\-probes
List the probes.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-probe " " \fIPROBE\fR
Run one probe and show what it reports, which is how a probe is debugged.
.
.SS detc bundle
Build, check and install a tree of objects.  A bundle is a signed tar archive
of variables, templates, resources, probes and providers that installs into the
.I run
prefixes, which is how a whole configuration reaches a machine as one file.
Installing one takes away the bundle installed before it.
.TP
.B create [\fIDIR\fB] \-o \fIOUTPUT\fB [\-\-sign \fIKEY\fB]
Build a bundle out of the source tree
.I DIR
(the current directory by default) and write it to
.IR OUTPUT ,
or to the standard output for
.IR \- .
.I KEY
is an SSH private key, and a bundle without one can only be installed by a
machine that was told to accept that.  A source tree that holds
.I variables/user.d
is refused, because that is where
.B detc var
writes: ship those documents as
.I variables/system.d
instead, which still wins over the distribution and still loses to whatever the
administrator sets.
.TP
.B verify \fIBUNDLE\fR
Check a bundle and everything it carries, without installing it.
.I BUNDLE
is a file,
.I \-
for the standard input, or an
.I http(s)://
URL.
.TP
.B install \fIBUNDLE\fR
Install it.  The signature is checked against
.I allowed_signers
of the tree being configured and nothing else, so a bundle cannot widen the
trust that admitted it.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-persist
Keep a copy, so that a reboot can put it back \(em the
.I run
prefixes do not survive one.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Apply the system once the bundle is installed.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-unsigned
Install a bundle that carries no signature.
.RE
.TP
.BR restore " [" \-\-apply ]
Install the kept copy again, which is what a machine does after a reboot.
.TP
.B status
Show the bundle the machine knows, and nothing when it knows none.  The last
field says whether it comes back after a reboot:
.B transient
for one that will not,
.B persistent
for one that a copy was kept of, and
.B kept
for the copy alone, which is the machine that has rebooted and has not been
restored yet.
.TP
.B remove
Take away the installed bundle, and the copy that was kept of it.  A bundle in
the
.B kept
state is taken away as well, which is how a machine whose restore keeps failing
stops arming it again at every boot.
.
.SS detc report [ID]
Show what a previous run did, out of the journal.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the runs there are.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-last
Show the last one.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-only\-fails
Show only what failed.
.
.SH THE JOURNAL
An applying run leaves two commits in a git repository at
.IR /var/lib/detc/journal.git :
.B found
is the state of the system as the run read it, and
.B applied
is the state it left behind.  The difference between two runs is therefore a
.BR git-diff (1),
and a change made outside
.B detc
shows up in the
.B found
commit of the next run.
.PP
The repository is mode 0700, because it holds rendered configuration files and
the whole namespace, and either can carry a password.  It has no remote and is
never pushed anywhere.
.PP
Three variables adjust it, and all three have defaults:
.IR detc.journal.enabled ,
.I detc.journal.user
and
.IR detc.journal.email .
Setting the first to false turns the history off.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything that was asked for succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Something failed, and said so on the standard error: a template that could not
be rendered, a provider that failed or is missing, an object that did not pass
.BR check .
A run that failed part way through has still applied and still recorded
everything that came before.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DETC_LOG_LEVEL
The log level, overriding
.BR \-d .
It takes the level, optionally per module
.RI ( detc::apply=debug ),
and optionally a
.I /text
suffix that keeps only the messages containing that text.
.TP
.B DETC_LOG_STYLE
Whether log lines are coloured:
.IR auto ,
.I always
or
.IR never .
.TP
.B DETC_ROOT
Set by
.B detc
for every probe and provider it runs, and never read by it.  It names the root
the run is about, which for a probe is the tree it must report on rather than
the one it is running under.
.TP
.B DETC_LIVE
Not read by
.B detc
either, and passed through to the same programs.  It says that the root is not
.I /
but the machine looking at it is the machine that will boot it, which is what
has the probes report this machine's addresses and disks for a tree that is
about to become it.  The initrd is the one caller that may say so.
.TP
.B DETC_RUN_LOCK
Set by
.BR detc ,
and by no caller.  It names the file an applying run holds an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on, released after the last journal commit and after
.IR last.yaml ,
so that
.RB \(lq "flock \(dq$DETC_RUN_LOCK\(dq \fICOMMAND\fB" \(rq
runs
.I COMMAND
once the run is really over.  It is set only while the lock is held, so
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
.B check
and
.B var
never name one, and a program that does not find it knows there is nothing to
wait for.
.
.SH FILES
Everything below is relative to
.BR \-\-root .
.TP
.IR /usr/share/detc/ {variables,templates.d,resources.d}
What the distribution ships.
.TP
.IR /usr/libexec/detc/ {probes,providers.d}
The executables it ships.
.TP
.IR /run/detc/ ", " /run/lib/detc/
Where an installed bundle lands, and what a reboot takes away.
.TP
.IR /etc/detc/ ", " /var/lib/detc/providers.d/
The administrator's own, which win over both.
.TP
.I /run/detc/variables/user.d/
Written by
.BR "detc var" ,
one document per key, named
.RI 95\- key .json
with the dots of the key turned into dashes.  A reboot takes it away.
.TP
.I /etc/detc/variables/user.d/
Written by
.B "detc var \-\-persist"
instead, named
.RI 90\- key .json
the same way.  The
.I 90\-
puts it after the documents written by hand in the usual
.I 50\-
range, and the
.I 95\-
of the one above puts that after this, so that a variable set now answers until
the boot that gives this one back.
.B "detc var \-\-unset"
takes both away, and nothing else in either directory.  Both directories are the
administrator's alone: a bundle cannot carry
.IR variables/user.d ,
and a variable is not set over a file that the installed bundle owns.
.TP
.IR /etc/detc/allowed_signers ", " /usr/share/detc/allowed_signers
Who may sign a bundle this machine will install, in the format of
.BR ssh-keygen (1)
.BR \-Y .
.TP
.I /var/lib/detc/journal.git
The history.
.TP
.I /var/lib/detc/last.yaml
What the last run did, which is what
.B report
reads when there is no journal.
.TP
.I /var/lib/detc/run.lock
The lock named by
.BR DETC_RUN_LOCK .
.TP
.IR /var/lib/detc/bundle.detc ", " /var/lib/detc/bundle.yaml
The bundle kept by
.BR \-\-persist ,
and what is known about it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/detc/providers/
Where a provider keeps what it recorded, one directory per type.  Not to be
confused with
.I /var/lib/detc/providers.d/
above: that one holds providers, this one holds what they wrote down.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set a variable, see what it does to a file, and then do it:
.PP
.EX
$ detc var \-k ssh.permit_root_login \-v prohibit\-password
$ detc cat /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60\-detc.conf
$ detc \-\-dry\-run apply
$ detc apply
.EE
.PP
Try the same thing against an image mounted elsewhere, changing nothing on this
machine:
.PP
.EX
$ detc \-\-root /mnt check
$ detc \-\-root /mnt \-\-dry\-run apply
.EE
.PP
Build a signed bundle out of a tree of objects and install it on a machine that
keeps it across a reboot:
.PP
.EX
$ detc bundle create ./fleet \-o fleet.detc \-\-sign ~/.ssh/detc_ca
$ detc bundle install fleet.detc \-\-persist \-\-apply
.EE
.PP
Take a template that the distribution ships out of the ladder, see that it is
gone, and put it back:
.PP
.EX
$ detc remove /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60\-detc.conf \-\-mask
$ detc list \-\-masked
$ detc unmask /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/60\-detc.conf
.EE
.PP
Read what an object says about itself, and what a type of resource accepts:
.PP
.EX
$ detc doc user/root
$ detc doc \-\-type provider user
$ detc schema user
.EE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR detcd (8),
.BR detctl (8),
.BR flock (1),
.BR ssh-keygen (1),
.BR dracut (8)
.PP
The guide, which is what this page is the short form of, is installed as
.I README.md
under
.IR /usr/share/doc/packages/detc/ .
Writing a probe, a provider, a template or a resource is documented beside it
under
.IR docs/authoring/ ,
and configuring a machine from its initrd in
.IR docs/detc\-inject.md .
